{
	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology\n\nDomain hub: hormonal and immune signalling between body and brain, including stress axes, autonomic control, circadian timing and neuroimmune interactions.\n\n[[Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-25; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 2100,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Axes and physiology",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 734
		},
		{
			"id": "s0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology - Axes and physiology|Axes and physiology index]]**",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[HPA axis]]**\n*process* - 8 sources\n\nThe hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis as a control loop: releasing factors, circulating hormones, feedback, and the timescales involved.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Stress response and the HPA axis]]**\n*mechanism* - 5 sources\n\nHPA axis anatomy and feedback, the contested role of glucocorticoids, the acute-to-chronic transition, and why single cortisol measures mislead.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 786,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Autonomic",
			"x": 420,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 758
		},
		{
			"id": "s1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology - Autonomic|Autonomic index]]**",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Autonomic measurement and heart rate variability]]**\n*method* - 4 sources\n\nHow autonomic function is measured, why heart rate variability has several definitions, and what physiological inferences are defensible.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 242,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Autonomic regulation]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nAutonomic divisions and central control, the limits of the fight-or-flight shorthand, and why heart rate variability is not one quantity.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 810,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g2",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Timing",
			"x": 840,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 734
		},
		{
			"id": "s2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology - Timing|Timing index]]**",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Circadian clock biology]]**\n*process* - 4 sources\n\nThe molecular clock, its coordination across tissues, and the outputs that connect biological timing to physiology and behaviour.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Circadian rhythms and sleep]]**\n*mechanism* - 4 sources\n\nCircadian clock biology, distributed tissue clocks, sleep pressure versus timing, and the limited evidence linking rhythm disruption to mood episodes.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 786,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g3",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Immune and barrier",
			"x": 1260,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1616
		},
		{
			"id": "s3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology - Immune and barrier|Immune and barrier index]]**",
			"x": 1290,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Allostasis and chronic stress]]**\n*theory* - 5 sources\n\nStability through change: the allostatic framework, its proposed consequences of repeated activation, and how it is measured.",
			"x": 1290,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Blood-brain barrier]]**\n*structure* - 4 sources\n\nThe components that separate blood from neural tissue, how selectivity is achieved, and why 'leaky barrier' claims need precision.",
			"x": 1290,
			"y": 786,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Cytokines and inflammation in psychiatric conditions]]**\n*theory* - 6 sources\n\nInflammatory signalling as a proposed contributor in a subset of cases, with the population-level evidence and its interpretation limits.",
			"x": 1290,
			"y": 1064,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 242,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Microbiome-gut-brain axis]]**\n*theory* - 4 sources\n\nGut microbiota as a proposed actor in brain function, the communication routes involved, and the causal limits of current evidence.",
			"x": 1290,
			"y": 1366,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neuroimmune interactions and microglia]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nMicroglia as the brain's resident immune cells, their surveillance and responses, and how immune signalling interacts with neural function.",
			"x": 1290,
			"y": 1644,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 242,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
